export const siteContent=[{"id":"site:about/index.html:0","title":"About Us","url":"/about/","kind":"website","body":"Athens and the Acropolis beneath an open sky\nOur story\nHope takes a step.\nWe believe meaningful travel can deepen faith, strengthen good work already underway, and change the way we serve long after returning home.\nBrent Kern smiling in a casual professional portrait\nBrent Kern\nFounder, pastor, traveler, and business leader\nMeet Brent\nExperience that connects purpose with practical action.\nBrent Kern brings 32 years of church service together with more than 28 years of corporate IT delivery and management experience. Across both settings, he has learned how to listen carefully, lead diverse teams, navigate complex challenges, and turn good intentions into practical next steps.\nHis ministry experience includes serving as a music pastor, interim pastor, children’s and youth director, and itinerant preacher. As the founder of Christian Steps Ministries, Brent continues to bring together Christian faith, teaching, service, and encouragement for people at different points in their journeys.\nBrent’s background also includes music and performance, youth sports, and extensive domestic and international travel. Those experiences have placed him alongside people of many ages, cultures, professions, and perspectives—preparation that shapes the welcoming, adaptable, and relationship-centered approach of Hope Sojourns.\nTravel has reinforced a simple conviction: the best service begins with humility. Hope Sojourns grows from Brent’s desire to help people cross cultures thoughtfully, learn from committed local leaders, and offer skills that meet real needs.\n“We each have previous steps, our current step, and future steps in our journey.”\nMeet with Brent\nA broad foundation\nExperience shaped by service, leadership, and curiosity.\nBrent’s diverse background helps him connect with people, adapt to unfamiliar settings, and guide teams with both purpose and practical care.\n01\nChurch and ministry\nThirty-two years serving churches as a music pastor, interim pastor, children’s and youth director, and itinerant preacher.\n02\nTechnology leadership\nMore than 28 years in corporate IT delivery and management, leading work, people, relationships, and change.\n03\nMusic and performance\nExperience spanning opera, musical theater, 1950s and 1960s doo-wop, barbershop quartet and chorus, contemporary and traditional church music, and concert bands.\n04\nYouth sports\nTen years coaching youth teams, along with umpiring experience at the youth, high-school, college, and semi-professional levels.\n05\nTravel and culture\nExtensive travel across 44 U.S. states, more than 20 countries, and five continents, with a continuing interest in people, culture, and place.\n06\nA versatile skill set\nA diverse mix of pastoral, technical, creative, operational, teaching, coaching, and relationship-building skills brought together in service of people and communities.\nChristian Steps Ministries logo\nChristian Steps Ministries\nOne practical step at a time.\nHope Sojourns works with Christian Steps Ministries, which helps people pursue a life of following Jesus through Christ-centered hope, Scripture-based teaching, and practical discipleship.\nVisit Christian Steps Ministries →","context":"About Us /about/"},{"id":"site:discover/index.html:0","title":"Follow your curiosity","url":"/discover/","kind":"website","body":"One encounter opens another\nWhat draws\nyou closer?\nYou might find more than one way to take part. Follow a question and see where it leads.\nI’d like to see the whole site ↗\nMake room for an encounter\nYou don’t have to arrive\nwith all the answers.\nBring your curiosity, your willingness to listen, and the part of your life you’re ready to share.\n01\nA few days, a new perspective\nExplore a short journey alongside a local ministry.\n↗02\nMore time to learn and serve\nConnect your skills and education with meaningful work.\n↗03\nPeople I’d love to bring\nImagine a shared experience for your community.\n↗\nThe people who stay\nThe work has a home.\nWe’re invited into it.\nLocal ministries know their communities. Their relationships help shape where we go and how we serve.\n01\nThis sounds like the work we’re doing.\nTell us about your ministry and the people you serve.\n↗02\nI’d like to help that work continue.\nExplore ways to support ongoing ministry and participation.\n↗03\nI want to experience it alongside them.\nExplore the journeys growing from these relationships.\n↗\nAn invitation you can share\nWho comes to mind\nwhen you imagine going?\nA few friends. Your church. Students you teach. The people you work beside every day.\n01\nI can picture people coming with me.\nExplore a journey for your church, workplace, or community.\n↗02\nI’m thinking about students and their future.\nConnect learning, practical skills, and service.\n↗03\nI could help make this possible.\nSupport the people and relationships behind a journey.\n↗","context":"Follow your curiosity /discover/"},{"id":"site:explore/index.html:0","title":"A world of people worth knowing","url":"/explore/","kind":"website","body":"Go with hope. Serve with faith.\nA world of people\nworth knowing.\nCome alongside local ministries. Make room for an encounter. Discover how service can change what you carry home.\nFind your next step↗Explore journeys↗\nSofia and Nikos of Hellenic Ministries with members of the Hope Sojourns team People first. Wherever we go.Athens · One of the places our relationships began\nAt the heart of every journey\nWe go to serve.\nWe make time to see.\nHope Sojourns brings willing people alongside ministries already caring for their communities. We begin by listening, contribute where we can, and stay open to what we might learn.Get to know us ↗\nWhere could this lead?\nFollow your curiosity.\nThere is more than one way to take part. You don’t need to have it figured out before you begin.\n01\nWhat might I discover?\nA few days away. A longer season of learning. A different perspective.\n↗02\nWhose work could I join?\nMeet the purpose behind local partnership and ongoing support.\n↗03\nWhat could we make possible together?\nImagine the invitation reaching your church, students, or community.\n↗\nPlaces to begin\nThe place is part\nof the story.\nExplore current and developing journeys. Each one grows from a local relationship, with its own people, needs, and possibilities.\nSome opportunities are still taking shape. See each journey for its dates, planning status, and details.\nHope Sojourns team members gathered on Mars Hill in Athens\nGreece\nAthens\nWalk alongside ministries serving neighbors experiencing homelessness, displacement, and exploitation.\nJune 26–July 2, 2027ScheduledSpots available · Pastoral planning trip\nJuly 10–20, 2027Team confirmedAdditional spots available\nExplore Athens →\nStudents in Kenya gathered outdoors\nEast Africa\nKenya\nEncourage a place of safety, education, and possibility for girls.\nExplore Kenya →\nThe loft at Cotton Wood Lodge in Belize where mission teams will stay\nCentral America\nBelize\nServe with a rainforest ministry rooted in relationship and community.\nMarch or April 2027Planning\nExplore Belize →\nA welcoming pedestrian street with cafes in Old Nice, France\nFrench Riviera\nNice\nHelp create joyful, welcoming Bible experiences for children and families.\nJuly 15–26, 2027ScheduledSpots available\nExplore Nice →\nThe Buffalo Conference Center at Shepherd of the Ozarks\nUnited States\nShepherd of the Ozarks\nSupport camp ministry and community needs near Marshall, Arkansas.\nNovember 2027Planning\nExplore Arkansas →\nPalacio de Bellas Artes and the Mexico City skyline\nMexico\nMexico City\nJoin Metro Relief of Dallas in supporting a sister ministry serving neighbors experiencing homelessness.\nJanuary 10–16, 2027ScheduledInterest conversations open\nExplore Mexico City →\nWhere hope leads next\nFuture Journeys\nDiscover new opportunities as trusted ministry relationships and community partnerships develop.\nSee what is ahead →\nConsidering more than one journey? Select every trip that interests you on one simple form.\nShare trip interest\nPrintable overview\nShare Hope Sojourns with your church, school, or team.\nThe brochure brings the mission, approach, featured journeys, internships, and group opportunities together in one easy-to-share resource.\n2-page PDF · 573 KB\nOpen and print brochure\nDownload the PDF ↓\nA view of Mexico City, one of our developing destinations New places. New perspective.\nGive your gifts room to grow\nWhat if learning\ntook you closer?\nConnect your education and skills with the daily work of a ministry. Explore a supported internship shaped around your learning and a partner’s needs.\nExplore internships↗Helping students find their next step?\nThe story behind the invitation\n“Brent… she wanted\na red juice box.”\nA small moment asked a much bigger question: had I really seen the person in front of me?\nRead Brent’s story↗\nBrent Kern\nBrent Kern\nFounder, Hope Sojourns\nAnother encounter: John’s story ↗","context":"A world of people worth knowing /explore/"},{"id":"site:giving/index.html:0","title":"Giving & Transparency","url":"/giving/","kind":"website","body":"Children and ministry partners gathered outside the Jerri Savuto Home for Girls in Kenya\nGive with clarity\nHelp hope go farther.\nYour generosity helps build responsible ministry partnerships, prepare people to serve, and strengthen work already underway.\nDonate now\nWhy giving matters\nA ministry built on trust.\nHope Sojourns connects willing people with trusted ministries and meaningful opportunities to serve. Gifts can help us develop partnerships, prepare teams and interns, provide scholarships, create training resources, and respond to needs identified by local leaders.\nTransparency is part of the ministry. We want every donor, traveler, church, and partner to understand who receives funds, what those funds support, and when a payment is a charitable gift rather than a purchase or trip payment.\nGive with confidence\nGifts are received by Christian Steps Ministries.\nChristian Steps Ministries is a registered 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization. All donations are tax-deductible to the extent allowed by law.\nEIN: 81-1678503\nView secure giving options\nSecure giving\nGive to Hope Sojourns.\nDonations are processed by Christian Steps Ministries in cooperation with Hope Sojourns. Choose a one-time, monthly, or yearly gift below. PayPal securely handles the transaction, with eligible donors also able to use Venmo or supported cards.\nYour gift will be identified in Christian Steps Ministries' records as originating from Hope Sojourns.\nChoose how often to give\nOne-Time\nMonthly\nYearly\nGift amount\n$\nUSD\nA single gift of $50.00.\nChoose a payment method to complete your gift\nLoading secure payment options…\nYour amount and schedule stay on this page. PayPal opens its secure checkout only when you are ready to authorize the gift.\nSecure checkout is temporarily unavailable here. Use the original PayPal giving page.\nGiving priorities\nThree ways generosity can serve.\nSpecific opportunities will be described before gifts are accepted so donors can make informed decisions.\n01\nBuild the ministry\nSupport partnership development, training, communications, safeguarding, administration, and the responsible growth of Hope Sojourns.\n02\nSend and prepare teams\nHelp provide participant scholarships, preparation resources, ministry supplies, local transportation, and other approved team needs.\n03\nStrengthen local partners\nContribute toward priorities identified by ministries already serving their communities, rather than imposing projects from outside.\nKnow the difference\nHow funds work.\nNot every payment connected with a mission trip is a charitable contribution.\nGive to the ministry\nAn unrestricted gift is received and controlled by Christian Steps Ministries and supports the ministry's overall mission. It does not reserve travel, purchase services, or provide a personal benefit.\nSupport a traveler\nSupport designated for a participant must follow the receiving organization's policies. The organization receiving charitable gifts must retain appropriate control over how those funds are used.\nPay for a trip\nTrip payments may cover airfare, lodging, meals, insurance, transportation, ministry expenses, and coordination. Paying for travel or receiving services is different from making an unrestricted gift.\nHire Hope Sojourns\nChurches, schools, ministries, or teams may purchase planning, training, internship, or coordination services. These service fees will be identified separately from charitable gifts and trip costs.\nA simple promise: We will not call a payment a donation when it is actually paying for travel or services. Charitable gifts are received by Christian Steps Ministries, a registered 501(c)(3), and are tax-deductible to the extent allowed by law.\nOur commitment\nTransparency donors can expect.\nClear ownership\nChristian Steps Ministries receives and controls charitable gifts supporting Hope Sojourns. EIN: 81-1678503.\nClear purpose\nWe will explain whether a gift is unrestricted, designated for a program, or connected with a specific ministry priority.\nSeparate costs\nDonations, trip payments, pass-through travel costs, and Hope Sojourns service fees will be described separately.\nRespectful reporting\nUpdates will focus on stewardship, partnership, and meaningful outcomes without exploiting the people or communities being served.\nHonest administration\nReasonable operating and leadership expenses will not be hidden. Sustainable ministry requires responsible administration.\nQuestions are welcome\nDonors and partners may ask how an opportunity is structured before deciding whether to participate.\nJoin the work\nHelp build Hope Sojourns.\nGive securely using the options above, or talk with Brent about the vision, current needs, and accountability plan.\nDonate nowTalk with Brent","context":"Giving & Transparency /giving/"},{"id":"site:groups/index.html:0","title":"Journeys for your community","url":"/groups/","kind":"website","body":"← Keep exploring\nShare the invitation\nA shared journey.\nA new conversation.\nBring people from your church, workplace, or community into an experience of listening and service.\nThe journey begins with who is coming, what they hope to learn, and how they could contribute alongside a local partner. Tell us what you imagine, and we’ll explore the possibilities together.\nLet’s imagine it together↗See current journeys ↗\nA conversation to begin\nBegin with your people\nWho you imagine coming with you\nWhat you hope to learn together\nThe time and resources you have\nStart with a question. We’ll work through the possibilities together.","context":"Journeys for your community /groups/"},{"id":"site:index.html:0","title":"Hope Sojourns","url":"/","kind":"website","body":"Athens spreading toward the Acropolis under a warm sky\nYou are Invited\nTravel farther. Serve closer.\nHope Sojourns brings willing people alongside trusted local ministries—creating room to listen, learn, encourage, and meet practical needs.\nExplore the journeys\nStudent internships\nLocally led\nRelationship centered\nPractical service\nOur approach\nNot mission tourism. Meaningful partnership.\nEvery journey begins with the priorities of people already serving their community. We arrive ready to contribute, not to take over.\nTry this now\nBegin with better questions.\nBefore choosing a destination, learn to notice the people, leadership, strengths, and priorities already present in a community.\nRead “Do You See Me?”\nWho is already serving faithfully?\nWhat do local leaders say is actually needed?\nWhat assumptions should I examine before arriving?\nFind your contribution\nBring useful skills, not a preset agenda.\nHope Sojourns matches willing people with practical work identified by trusted ministry partners.\nExplore current journeys\nHospitality and outreach\nChildren and youth\nFacilities and projects\nStorytelling and communications\nPastoral and ministry support\nAfter the journey\nLet the experience reshape ordinary life.\nA meaningful journey continues through reflection, faithful storytelling, ongoing support, and service at home.\nExplore journey resources\nReflect on what challenged or changed you.\nShare stories that honor people, not your role.\nChoose one practice of service to continue.\nUpcoming possibilities\nJourneys with a reason to go.\nThese holder pages introduce developing opportunities. Dates, costs, team roles, and final partner plans will be confirmed as each journey takes shape.\nYou are Invited.\nGo with Hope. Serve with Faith.\nHope Sojourns team members gathered on Mars Hill in Athens\nGreece\nAthens\nWalk alongside ministries serving neighbors experiencing homelessness, displacement, and exploitation.\nJune 26–July 2, 2027ScheduledSpots available · Pastoral planning trip\nJuly 10–20, 2027Team confirmedAdditional spots available\nExplore Athens →\nStudents in Kenya gathered outdoors\nEast Africa\nKenya\nEncourage a place of safety, education, and possibility for girls.\nExplore Kenya →\nThe loft at Cotton Wood Lodge in Belize where mission teams will stay\nCentral America\nBelize\nServe with a rainforest ministry rooted in relationship and community.\nMarch or April 2027Planning\nExplore Belize →\nA welcoming pedestrian street with cafes in Old Nice, France\nFrench Riviera\nNice\nHelp create joyful, welcoming Bible experiences for children and families.\nJuly 15–26, 2027ScheduledSpots available\nExplore Nice →\nThe Buffalo Conference Center at Shepherd of the Ozarks\nUnited States\nShephard of the Ozarks\nSupport camp ministry and community needs near Marshall, Arkansas.\nNovember 2027Planning\nExplore Arkansas →\nPalacio de Bellas Artes and the Mexico City skyline\nMexico\nMexico City\nJoin Metro Relief of Dallas in supporting a sister ministry serving neighbors experiencing homelessness.\nJanuary 10–16, 2027ScheduledInterest conversations open\nExplore Mexico City →\nWhere hope leads next\nFuture Journeys\nDiscover new opportunities as trusted ministry relationships and community partnerships develop.\nSee what is ahead →\nConsidering more than one journey? Select every trip that interests you on one simple form.\nShare trip interest\nPrintable overview\nShare Hope Sojourns with your church, school, or team.\nThe brochure brings the mission, approach, featured journeys, internships, and group opportunities together in one easy-to-share resource.\n2-page PDF · 573 KB\nOpen and print brochure\nDownload the PDF ↓\nFor students and graduates\nTurn your field of study into service.\nExplore developing internships where ministry, communications, operations, travel, and community partnership meet.\nExplore internships\nMinistry & community engagementStorytelling & communicationsProgram & trip operationsDigital media & outreach\nChristian Steps Ministries logo\nIn partnership\nWalking with Christian Steps Ministries.\nHope Sojourns works with Christian Steps Ministries, an organization dedicated to helping people take practical next steps in following Jesus.\nVisit Christian Steps Ministries →","context":"Hope Sojourns /"},{"id":"site:interest/index.html:0","title":"Share Your Interest","url":"/interest/","kind":"website","body":"Athens spreading toward the Acropolis under a warm sky\nTake the next step\nTell us where hope may be leading you.\nChoose every trip or internship you would like to explore. This is an expression of interest, not a commitment.\nOne form, several possibilities\nYou do not have to choose just one.\nSelect as many current trips and internship placements as you want. Adults in every season of life are welcome, and no school affiliation is required. We will keep one contact record for you and connect each distinct opportunity to it.\nInterest received\nThank you for taking the next step.\nA Hope Sojourns team member will follow up with you.\nReturn home\nBook a conversation\nWhat happens next\nInterest first. Registration follows.\nWe will begin with a conversation about fit, timing, expectations, and current partner needs. When a particular trip is ready for registration, participants will receive a separate secure application.\nWhy two steps?\nThe interest form stays simple. The later registration process can carefully handle travel details, agreements, and other information that needs stronger privacy and review.","context":"Share Your Interest /interest/"},{"id":"site:internships/index.html:0","title":"Internships","url":"/internships/","kind":"website","body":"Students gathered outdoors in Kenya\nCollege students & graduates\nBring your gifts. Grow your calling.\nDevelop practical experience where service, culture, faith, and vocation meet.\nSee internship possibilities\nHow the program works\nA supported ministry internship\nPut your education to work in a place where people matter.\nHope Sojourns connects students with developing ministry placements and stays involved from the first conversation through the final reflection. Together, we shape an experience around your academic goals, your gifts, and a ministry’s genuine needs.\n01\nA thoughtful match\nWe learn about your major, interests, availability, and goals before exploring a fit with a ministry partner.\n02\nPreparation that matters\nBefore you leave, we work through expectations, culture, service, travel, communication, and personal safety.\n03\nSupport while you serve\nYou receive local ministry supervision and a weekly Hope Sojourns check-in for reflection, encouragement, and problem-solving.\nInternship pathways\nReal work with room to learn.\nHope Sojourns is developing flexible opportunities for students and recent graduates. Placements will be shaped around partner needs, a participant’s skills, and responsible supervision.\n+ Select any pathway to explore ways your education could contribute.\n01\nMinistry & community engagement\nSupport relationship-building, volunteer coordination, children’s programming, hospitality, and community outreach.\nExplore this pathway\nWays you could contribute\nWelcome guests and help create thoughtful hospitality experiences.\nAssist with community, children’s, youth, or volunteer programming.\nLearn culturally humble approaches to listening and relationship-building.\nEspecially relevant for: ministry, social work, education, sociology, psychology, and human services.\n02\nCommunications & storytelling\nHelp document impact responsibly through writing, interviews, photography, newsletters, and partner updates.\nExplore this pathway\nWays you could contribute\nInterview ministry leaders and turn conversations into clear, respectful stories.\nCreate photo, video, newsletter, or partner-update content.\nBuild a portfolio while practicing ethical, dignity-centered storytelling.\nEspecially relevant for: communications, journalism, photography, videography, public relations, and English.\n03\nProgram & trip operations\nBuild experience in planning, research, logistics, participant preparation, documentation, and follow-through.\nExplore this pathway\nWays you could contribute\nResearch travel, schedules, supplies, and on-the-ground logistics.\nPrepare participant resources, checklists, and project documentation.\nHelp teams turn lessons learned into stronger future programs.\nEspecially relevant for: hospitality, tourism, event planning, operations, project management, and organizational leadership.\n04\nDigital media & outreach\nContribute to web content, social media, design, video, and digital campaigns that invite others into service.\nExplore this pathway\nWays you could contribute\nPlan and produce useful content for web, email, and social channels.\nCreate digital assets that help people understand opportunities and take a next step.\nUse audience insights to improve clarity, reach, and engagement.\nEspecially relevant for: marketing, graphic design, web development, media production, and digital communications.\n05\nBusiness & nonprofit practice\nApply project management, process improvement, budgeting, partner care, and organizational skills in a ministry context.\nExplore this pathway\nWays you could contribute\nMap processes and create tools that make recurring work easier.\nSupport budgeting, project tracking, partner communication, or impact reporting.\nStudy how mission, stewardship, people, and operations work together.\nEspecially relevant for: business, accounting, finance, nonprofit management, entrepreneurship, and human resources.\n06\nCustom academic placement\nExplore an arrangement connected to your major, capstone, practicum, or graduate interests when supervision and partner fit allow.\nExplore this pathway\nWays a custom placement can take shape\nConnect academic learning objectives with a genuine partner need.\nDefine supervision, responsibilities, and a meaningful final deliverable.\nCoordinate school approval or practicum requirements when possible.\nA strong starting point: bring your program requirements, ideal dates, learning goals, and the skills you hope to practice.\nDeveloping possibilities\nFive places to put learning into practice.\nThese opportunities are starting points for a conversation. Dates, responsibilities, travel, costs, supervision, and partner fit will be confirmed with each participant.\nInternational1–9 months\n01\nEurope\nAthens, Greece\nNew Start MinistriesCompassionate outreach and practical support for women affected by trafficking and sexual exploitation, centered on dignity, safety, and restoration.","context":"Internships /internships/"},{"id":"site:internships/index.html:1","title":"Internships","url":"/internships/","kind":"website","body":"Glocal CafeHelp refugees navigate social and medical support and access food, clothing, and other practical resources.\nEngagement1 to 9 months\nI’m interested →\nInternational1–4 months\n02\nEurope\nEngland\nMinistry partnerThe Light Group\nPotential experienceA developing ministry placement shaped around partner needs, your field of study, and appropriate supervision.\nEngagement1 to 4 months\nI’m interested →\nInternational2–6 months\n03\nNorth America\nMexico City, Mexico\nMinistry partnerGod’s Kitchen\nPotential experienceServe people experiencing homelessness, displaced families and refugees, and individuals rebuilding their lives after addiction.\nEngagement2 to 6 months\nI’m interested →\nUnited States1–3 months\n04\nNorthern Arkansas\nShephard of the Ozarks\nSOTO Arkansas\nPotential experienceSupport Christian camp programming and learn alongside staff caring for camp animals, wooded property, and outdoor ministry spaces.\nEngagement1 to 3 months\nI’m interested →\nUnited States1–3 months\n05\nDallas, Texas\nMetro Relief of Dallas\nPotential experienceLearn alongside case workers, support grant development, and participate in relationship-centered outreach with people experiencing homelessness.\nEngagement1 to 3 months\nI’m interested →\nHope Sojourns\nInternships\nYour next step\nWhich place and pathway fit your goals?\nShare your field of study, preferred location, available dates, and what you hope to learn. We’ll explore where your preparation and a partner’s needs may meet.\nShare internship interest\nFrom interest to impact\nYou will not navigate the experience alone.\nEvery placement is developed individually. These steps help us protect the learning experience, serve the ministry well, and prepare you for the realities of living and working in a new setting.\n01\nShare your interests\nTell us about your school, major, timing, preferred location, and what you hope to learn.\n02\nExplore the fit\nHope Sojourns and the ministry learn more about you while you learn about the role, setting, and expectations.\n03\nCoordinate with your school\nIf academic credit is involved, your college or university determines eligibility, assignments, required hours, and grading.\n04\nPrepare well\nWe meet before departure to cover your learning plan, cultural humility, ethical service, travel, safety, and communication.\n05\nArrive with support\nWhen included in your placement plan, a Hope Sojourns leader travels with you to help you get situated and connected locally.\n06\nServe, learn, and reflect\nYour ministry supervisor guides the daily work. Hope Sojourns meets with you weekly and stays connected with your school when appropriate.\nPlanning responsibly\nKnow what support is included—and what you will need to plan for.\nCosts vary by destination, duration, ministry support, and travel. Before you commit, Hope Sojourns will provide a written placement plan and estimated budget.\nHope Sojourns support\nYour program experience\nPlacement coordination and school collaboration\nPre-departure preparation meetings\nWeekly check-ins throughout your internship\nArrival support and leader accompaniment when included\nOngoing communication with your ministry and school\nYour budget\nStudent responsibilities\nCollege tuition and fees, if required by your school\nYour airfare and personal travel costs\nHousing, food, and local transportation unless the local ministry provides or subsidizes them\nPassport, visa, insurance, background check, and personal expenses\nA Hope Sojourns Program Support Fee and any disclosed leader travel share\nPartner support varies\nSome ministries help reduce living costs.\nDepending on the location, a ministry may offer a place to stay, meals or food support, local transportation, or a stipend. We will confirm exactly what is available before you make a decision.\nAsk about a placement →\nWhat to expect\nA formation experience—not just a résumé line.\nA clear learning plan and defined responsibilities\nRegular reflection on faith, vocation, culture, and ethical service\nCoaching from experienced ministry and business leaders\nWork designed around genuine organizational needs\nA final portfolio, presentation, or learning summary when appropriate\nYour school remains in charge of academic credit. Hope Sojourns helps coordinate the placement and communication, while your college or university sets academic requirements, assignments, hours, and grades.\nStart with a conversation\nTell us about your school, field of study, timing, travel interests, and what you hope to learn.\nDiscuss an internship\nHave a question?\nAsk about an internship, ministry, video, or photo using the question buttons. Browse reviewed questions and answers, or leave your contact details for Brent to follow up.","context":"Internships /internships/"},{"id":"site:partners/index.html:0","title":"Local ministry partnerships","url":"/partners/","kind":"website","body":"← Keep exploring\nStart with your community\nWhat could we build\nalongside you?\nYou know the people you serve. We’d like to hear about your work and where a partnership might be useful.\nA visiting team or an intern should contribute to the work you are already doing. Your priorities, capacity, and community help shape that possibility.\nTalk about a partnership↗Explore internship possibilities ↗\nA conversation to begin\nBegin with your perspective\nWhat your community needs\nWhat visitors or interns could contribute\nWhat a sustainable relationship requires\nStart with a question. We’ll work through the possibilities together.","context":"Local ministry partnerships /partners/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/athens-2025/index.html:0","title":"Athens 2025","url":"/past-trips/athens-2025/","kind":"website","body":"The Athens 2025 mission team at the Parthenon\nAthens, Greece · 2025\nServe people, not projects.\nA journey shaped by local partnerships, attentive service, and the dignity found in truly seeing another person.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse this page’s photos, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe journey\nListening before leading.\nOur team served alongside churches and ministry partners caring for refugees, people experiencing homelessness, and vulnerable neighbors throughout Athens. Practical help mattered, but relationships gave that work its meaning.\nWe spent time with Sofia and Nikos of Hellenic Ministries of Athens, whose local knowledge helped our visiting team understand the community more deeply. We also gathered with the Albanian Church of Athens and met people faithfully serving across cultures and languages.\nShe wanted a red one.\nDuring homeless outreach, a woman asked for a red juice box. We had a blue one available, and the difference initially seemed unimportant. Then a teammate quietly reminded us: she wanted a red one.\nThat small request became a lasting lesson. People are not tasks to finish. They have preferences, stories, and God-given dignity. Humble service slows down long enough to notice the person in front of us.\nFaith, history, and place.\nThe Acropolis, Hadrian’s Arch, the original Olympic Stadium in Athens, the Corinth Canal, Aegina Island, and Ancient Corinth provided space to learn and reflect. Together, ministry and cultural experiences helped us return home with a deeper commitment to listen first and serve with care.\nView all Athens 2025 photos\nAll past trips →\nTrip at a glance\nDestinationAthens, Greece\nYear2025\nLocal connectionsHellenic Ministries of Athens and the Albanian Church of Athens\nLasting lessonHonor each person through attentive, relationship-centered service.\nScenes from the journey\nAthens 2025\nA gathering at the Albanian Church of Athens Albanian Church of AthensA gathering with the local church community.\nSofia and Nikos of Hellenic Ministries of Athens with mission team members Hellenic Ministries of AthensSofia and Nikos with members of our visiting team.\nMars Hill in Athens Mars HillA view from this historic place in Athens.\nThe mission team at the Parthenon The mission teamTogether at the Parthenon.","context":"Athens 2025 /past-trips/athens-2025/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/athens-2026/index.html:0","title":"Athens 2026","url":"/past-trips/athens-2026/","kind":"website","body":"The 2026 Athens mission team\nAthens, Greece · June 3–15, 2026\nServing together in Athens.\nTwelve travelers from three states joined local ministries in practical service, prayer, and relationship.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse this page’s photos, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe journey\nArriving ready to listen and serve.\nA team of 12 people from Texas, Colorado, and Wisconsin traveled to Athens to serve alongside local ministries and care for people throughout the city.\nThe team worked with local churches and connected with neighbors experiencing homelessness, refugees, and people vulnerable to trafficking. Their service included food, essential supplies, prayer, conversation, children’s activities, worship, and helping people feel seen and valued.\nDonations fully covered the trip. The wider journey also included family travel through Switzerland, Austria, and Italy before the mission work began in Greece.\nView the complete journey gallery\nTrip at a glance\nDatesJune 3–15, 2026Team12 people from Texas, Colorado, and WisconsinServiceChurches, homeless outreach, refugee ministry, and support for vulnerable peopleArchiveHope Sojourns past trips","context":"Athens 2026 /past-trips/athens-2026/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/gallery/index.html:0","title":"Past Trip Photo Gallery","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"website","body":"Vintage world map with dotted travel routes and airplanes\nPast trip archive\nPhoto Gallery\nExplore photographs from completed journeys, gathered in one place.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse the photos shown, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe journeys in pictures\nPeople, places, and moments along the way.\nImages are served from the canonical Christian Steps Ministries archive so both sites stay in sync.\nLoading trip photographs…","context":"Past Trip Photo Gallery /past-trips/gallery/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/index.html:0","title":"Past Trips","url":"/past-trips/","kind":"website","body":"The 2026 mission team serving in Athens, Greece\nStories from the road\nPast trips\nLook back on journeys that taught us to listen first, serve with humility, and carry what we learned home.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse this page’s photos, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe journey so far\nEvery trip leaves a next step.\nThese summaries are drawn from the Christian Steps Ministries trip archive.\nBrowse every trip photo →\nStories from our trips\nLoading trip stories…\nFrom the archives\nThe Acropolis in Athens\nGreece · 2025\nAthens\nA formative journey that changed how Brent understood dignity, humility, and what it means to truly see the people we serve.\nRead the trip story →\nThe 2026 Athens mission team\nGreece · June 3–15, 2026\nAthens\nTwelve people served alongside local ministries, churches, refugees, neighbors experiencing homelessness, and people vulnerable to trafficking.\nRead the trip story →\nThe Buffalo Conference Center at Shepherd of the Ozarks\nArkansas · July 27–30, 2026\nShepherd of the Ozarks\nA planning visit centered on listening, learning, and preparing future Hope Sojourns work teams to serve SOTO well.\nRead the trip story →\nMission team members with a child in Kenya\nKenya · June 2018\nKenya\nA journey through Nairobi, Maua, Samburu, Maasai Mara, and Lake Nakuru centered on devotionals, practical service, relationships, and wonder.\nRead the trip story →","context":"Past Trips /past-trips/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/kenya-2018/index.html:0","title":"Kenya 2018","url":"/past-trips/kenya-2018/","kind":"website","body":"A scattered pile of printed photographs from the Kenya 2018 mission trip\nKenya · June 2018\nFaith made practical in Kenya.\nTeaching, building, friendship, and wonder carried us from Maua to the open landscapes of Kenya.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse this page’s photos, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe journey\nServing with our words, hands, and presence.\nOur 2018 journey took us through Nairobi, Maua, Samburu, Maasai Mara, and Lake Nakuru. Each place offered a different view of Kenya and a different invitation to listen, learn, serve, and receive the welcome of the people we met.\nIn Maua, we taught devotionals and shared encouragement with the community. We also worked alongside local partners at a hospital compound, helping build a new perimeter wall. The wall was a practical safety project intended to protect patients and staff by preventing unauthorized access to the hospital grounds.\nBeyond the construction work, we met and played with local children. Laughter, games, conversation, and time together became some of the journey’s most meaningful moments. They reminded us that service is not only measured by a completed project, but also by whether we slow down enough to notice people and share life with them.\nSeveral safaris brought us face to face with giraffes, elephants, lions, antelope, ostriches, topi, hyenas, and broad herds moving across the landscape. Those experiences gave us time to rest, reflect, and marvel at the beauty and diversity of God’s creation.\nKenya 2018 brought ministry and discovery together: devotionals offered in faith, a wall built for safety, friendships formed through play and presence, and a renewed sense of wonder carried home.\nView the Kenya photo gallery\nTrip at a glance\nDateJune 2018PlacesNairobi, Maua, Samburu, Maasai Mara, and Lake NakuruServiceDevotionals, hospital safety-wall construction, and time with local childrenJourneyService, cultural learning, and wildlife safaris","context":"Kenya 2018 /past-trips/kenya-2018/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/kenya-2019/index.html:0","title":"Kenya Trip Moved","url":"/past-trips/kenya-2019/","kind":"website","body":"Kenya 2018\nThis trip took place in 2018. Continue to the corrected trip page.","context":"Kenya Trip Moved /past-trips/kenya-2019/"},{"id":"site:past-trips/soto-arkansas-2026/index.html:0","title":"SOTO Arkansas 2026","url":"/past-trips/soto-arkansas-2026/","kind":"website","body":"The Buffalo Conference Center at Shepherd of the Ozarks\nArkansas · July 27–30, 2026\nPreparing the way to serve.\nA four-day visit built around listening, learning, prayer, and careful preparation.\nPhoto viewer: Select any photo to see the entire image. Use Previous and Next to browse this page’s photos, or the × (X) button to close.\nThe purpose\nPlanning future Hope Sojourns work teams.\nA planning team traveled to Shepherd of the Ozarks (SOTO) to see the ministry firsthand, meet the people serving there, and understand its facilities, work, and practical needs.\nThe visit helped determine how and when Hope Sojourns can begin sending work teams to SOTO. Time was spent walking the grounds, discussing potential projects, and considering the skills, tools, team sizes, and seasons that future service may require.\nThe goal is not simply to arrive with willing hands. It is to build relationships, understand the work that will truly help, and prepare volunteers to serve side by side with the people already carrying SOTO’s mission.\nView trip photos\nTrip at a glance\nDatesJuly 27–30, 2026DestinationShepherd of the Ozarks, ArkansasPurposePlan useful, well-prepared Hope Sojourns work teamsArchiveHope Sojourns past trips","context":"SOTO Arkansas 2026 /past-trips/soto-arkansas-2026/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:0","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"Athens beneath a warm sky\nSeeing People\nDo You See Me?\nLearning to See People the Way Jesus Does\nArticleBrent D KernAugust 9, 2026Download PDF ↓\nWatch and listen\nContinue the story through music and teaching.\nOfficial music video\nSet to the reflective song written by @PapaOdin and Brent D Kern, this official music video by Brent D Kern carries the article’s central invitation into images: look beyond labels, notice the person before you, and learn to see with the compassion of Jesus.\nWatch on YouTube →\nCompanion sermon\nIn this companion message, Brent D Kern expands on the article through Scripture and mission experiences, asking what changes when followers of Jesus stop, listen, draw near, and recognize the God-given dignity of people who are often overlooked.\nYour browser does not support audio playback. Open the sermon audio.\nOpen sermon audio →\nCompanion song\nListen as you read.\nDo You See Me?, written by @PapaOdin and Brent D Kern, was created to accompany this article and sermon.\nYour browser does not support audio playback. Open the song.\nSelect Play to begin the companion song.\nThere is a difference between looking at a person and truly seeing them.\nThere is a difference between looking at a person and truly seeing them.\nWe look at people every day. We pass them in grocery stores, sit beside them in waiting rooms, drive past them at intersections, work alongside them, worship beside them, and scroll past their pictures on our phones. We see faces constantly.\nBut how often do we actually see the person?\nThat question has become increasingly important to me through mission work, particularly during two trips to Athens, Greece. In Athens I encountered refugees, people experiencing homelessness, immigrants, people struggling with addiction, and people who simply seemed lost in a city where thousands of people walked past them every day.\nI went there intending to serve. Instead, God began teaching me how to see. And one of the first lessons was uncomfortable.\nThe God Who Sees\nHagar may be one of Scripture's clearest examples. She was an Egyptian servant in Abraham and Sarah's household. When Sarah could not have children, Hagar became caught in the consequences of decisions made by other people. She became pregnant with Abraham's child, and the relationship between Sarah and Hagar deteriorated badly.\nEventually Hagar fled into the wilderness. She was a foreigner, a servant, a woman with little social power, pregnant, alone, and running away. Yet in the wilderness, God found her.\nThe angel of the Lord found Hagar near a spring in the desert; it was the spring that is beside the road to Shur. And he said, “Hagar, slave of Sarai, where have you come from, and where are you going?”\n“I'm running away from my mistress Sarai,” she answered.\nThen the angel of the Lord told her, “Go back to your mistress and submit to her.” The angel added, “I will increase your descendants so much that they will be too numerous to count.”\nThe angel of the Lord also said to her: “You are now pregnant and you will give birth to a son. You shall name him Ishmael, for the Lord has heard of your misery.”\nShe gave this name to the Lord who spoke to her: “You are the God who sees me,” for she said, “I have now seen the One who sees me.”\nGenesis 16:7–13 (NIV)\nWhat an extraordinary name for God: the God who sees me. Hagar may have felt invisible to everyone around her. She was not invisible to God.\nGod did not merely see a pregnant servant beside a spring. He saw Hagar. He knew where she had been, what had happened to her, where she was going, and her misery. He called her by name.\nThat truth should shape the way followers of Jesus see other people. If we worship the God who sees, should we not become people who see?\nThree Men Saw Him. Only One Truly Saw Him.\nJesus develops this idea powerfully in the story we know as the Good Samaritan.\nAn expert in the law asked Jesus, “What must I do to inherit eternal life?” Jesus pointed him to the commands to love God with all his heart, soul, strength, and mind, and to love his neighbor as himself. Wanting to justify himself, the man asked, “And who is my neighbor?”\nJesus told of a man attacked by robbers and left half dead. A priest came down the road and, when he saw the man, passed by on the other side. A Levite also came, saw him, and passed by. But a Samaritan came where the man was and, when he saw him, took pity on him. He bandaged the man's wounds, brought him to an inn, cared for him, and paid for his continued care.\nLuke 10:25–35 (NIV, condensed)\nThe language is striking. The priest saw him. The Levite saw him. The Samaritan saw him. So who actually saw the man?\nAll three saw a body beside the road. Only one allowed what he saw to become compassion. Only one moved closer, touched the wounded man, interrupted his journey, spent his resources, and treated the wounded stranger as though his life mattered.\nThat is the difference between looking and seeing. True seeing changes something in us.","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:1","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"Perhaps that is what it takes to truly see someone. We see with our heart. We see with our soul. We see with our strength. We see with our mind. We see because we love.\nShe Wanted a Red One\nIn July 2025, I traveled to Athens on what I consider my first official mission trip. I had participated in service projects before and had certainly done work in the name of Jesus, but this was different. We had intentionally traveled somewhere for the purpose of serving people and sharing the love of Christ.\nOne evening our team walked through Athens distributing food to refugees and people experiencing homelessness. We carried sandwiches, chocolate, and small juice boxes. Some of the juice boxes were blue. Others were red.\nI approached a refugee woman who did not speak English and offered her a sandwich and some chocolate. Then I handed her a blue juice box. She immediately shook her finger. “No, no, no!”\nOur guide explained that she wanted a red juice box. I looked through my bag. I did not have any red ones left. She continued insisting that she wanted red.\nInternally, my attitude began to change. I remember thinking how ridiculous the situation seemed. We were walking through the city giving away food and drinks. Everything was free. Why should the color of the juice box matter? My attitude became something like: *You get what you get. You should be grateful for what we are giving you.* Eventually she walked away without a drink because she would not take the blue one.\nLater that evening our team gathered to talk about the day. When it was my turn, I told everyone about the encounter. I told it almost as though the woman's behavior demonstrated how unreasonable people could be.\nOne of the women on our team listened and then said something simple. “Brent, you don't understand. She wanted a red one.” I responded, “I know. She wanted a red one.” Then she said it again: “Brent. She wanted a red one.”\nSomething changed in me. Suddenly I understood. The issue had never really been the juice box. The issue was that I had stopped seeing the woman.\nSomewhere in my thinking, she had become a category instead of a person: refugee, hungry person, someone receiving charity. I had unconsciously placed myself above her. I was the person with something to give, and she was the person who ought to gratefully accept whatever I chose to hand her.\nBut she was not merely “a refugee.” She was a woman. She had preferences, memories, fears, hopes, things she liked and things she disliked. She probably had family members she loved and people she missed. She had experienced happiness and disappointment. She had a personality. She had a story. Just like me.\nAnd on that particular evening, she wanted a red juice box. It was such a small thing, yet it exposed something significant in me. I had looked directly at her without truly seeing her.\nJesus Saw the Crowd—and the People Inside It\nJesus went through all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, proclaiming the good news of the kingdom and healing every disease and sickness. When he saw the crowds, he had compassion on them, because they were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd. Then he said to his disciples, “The harvest is plentiful but the workers are few. Ask the Lord of the harvest, therefore, to send out workers into his harvest field.”\nMatthew 9:35–38 (NIV)\nJesus saw crowds differently than we do. We tend to turn crowds into statistics: hundreds, thousands, millions; refugees, homeless populations, prisoners, migrants, poor communities, the uninsured, the addicted, the elderly, the grieving.\nStatistics can help us understand the scale of suffering, but they can also unintentionally hide the people inside the numbers. Jesus could look at a crowd and somehow never lose the individual. Matthew does not merely say Jesus observed a large number of people. He saw them. He understood that they were harassed and helpless. And when Jesus saw them, He had compassion.\nThat pattern appears repeatedly throughout His ministry: Jesus saw. Jesus understood. Jesus had compassion. Jesus responded. The question for us is whether we are willing to do the same.\nThe Loneliness of Being Invisible\nI returned to Athens in June 2026. During another evening feeding walk, I met a man from England whom I will call John. He had been living homeless in Greece for more than twenty years.\nAfter giving him some food and something to drink, I began talking with him. The rest of our team needed to continue the feeding walk, but they encouraged me to stay. They would come back for me later. So I sat and talked with John.\nWhen the group returned, I asked whether I could pray for him. He allowed me to. Later our guide, Artur, told me that John normally did not invite people into his life that way.","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:2","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"The following evening we went out again. Before we started, I told Artur, “I want to find John.” We found him sitting on the park bench he jokingly called his “office.” This time I stayed for about an hour.\nWe talked—not ministry-talk, and not a carefully constructed evangelistic presentation. We simply talked like two human beings. John told me about his life. I learned how he survived, about his nightly routine of finding food and hoping for enough money for a glass of wine, about his distrust of governments and authority, and about his former wife, his daughter, and his grandchildren.\nFor an hour, the man people walked past every day was not merely a homeless man sitting on a bench. He was John.\nEventually I had to leave. As I stood to go, I told him, “John, I am going to try to come back and see you again this week. But until then, please take care of yourself.” His response has stayed with me ever since. He told me that taking care of himself was never really the problem. The real problem was the loneliness.\nJohn sat beside a path traveled by thousands of people. People saw him every day. Some probably gave him money. Others gave him food. Some hurried around him. Others deliberately avoided eye contact. Thousands of eyes saw someone sitting on that bench. But how many people actually saw John?\nFor that hour, I had an opportunity to see him. But something else happened too. John allowed me to see him. He became vulnerable. He shared pieces of himself that he could have kept hidden. And as we talked, he saw me too.\nThat is part of what makes genuine human connection so powerful. I saw him. He allowed himself to be seen. He saw me. And I allowed myself to be seen. For a little while, neither of us was a category. We were simply two people sitting together on a bench.\nThe Bible is filled with people longing to see and to be seen. Moses longed to see God. Thomas wanted to see evidence before he could believe. People repeatedly asked Jesus for signs. But Scripture also reveals something even more profound: before we ever begin searching for God, God sees us.\nJesus Stopped\nAs Jesus and his disciples, together with a large crowd, were leaving Jericho, a blind man named Bartimaeus was sitting by the roadside begging. Hearing that Jesus was passing, he shouted, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Many rebuked him and told him to be quiet, but he shouted all the more.\nJesus stopped and said, “Call him.” When Bartimaeus came, Jesus asked, “What do you want me to do for you?”\n“Rabbi, I want to see,” he said.\n“Go,” Jesus replied, “your faith has healed you.” Immediately he received his sight and followed Jesus along the road.\nMark 10:46–52 (NIV, condensed)\nMark prepares us for this encounter with a striking contrast. Just before Bartimaeus appears, James and John ask Jesus for positions of honor (Mark 10:35–45). Jesus asks them, “What do you want me to do for you?” A few verses later, He asks Bartimaeus exactly the same question. James and John ask for status. Bartimaeus asks for sight. The disciples can see Jesus with their eyes, yet they still misunderstand the road of sacrifice and service He is traveling. Bartimaeus, though physically blind, recognizes Jesus as the Son of David and follows Him along the road.\nEven his name adds meaning to the encounter. Bartimaeus means “son of Timaeus,” and the name Timaeus carries the sense of being honored or valued. Yet this “son of the honored one” is sitting beside the road as a dishonored man—a blind beggar whom the crowd attempts to silence. The crowd treats him as an interruption. Jesus stops, calls him forward, listens to him, and publicly recognizes his faith. Jesus sees the honor and value in the man others have dismissed.\nTwo words in that story deserve our attention: Jesus stopped. There was a crowd. There was noise. Jesus was traveling somewhere. The people around Bartimaeus considered him an interruption and told him to be quiet. But Jesus stopped.\nThe crowd heard noise. Jesus heard a person. The crowd saw a beggar. Jesus saw Bartimaeus.\nThe crowd’s response echoes the priest and the Levite in Jesus’ story. They encounter a suffering person, but they do not allow his need to interrupt their journey. They create distance—this time by trying to silence him. Jesus responds as the Samaritan did. He allows another person’s need to interrupt His journey. He stops, calls Bartimaeus closer, listens to him, and responds with mercy. The crowd sees an interruption beside the road. Jesus sees a neighbor.\nThen Jesus asked an interesting question: “What do you want me to do for you?” The need might have seemed obvious, but Jesus allowed Bartimaeus to speak. He gave him dignity. He let the man describe his own need.\nSometimes seeing people means resisting the temptation to decide who they are and what they need before we have listened to them. Sometimes seeing means stopping. Sometimes it means asking. Sometimes it means listening.","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:3","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"Jesus did not hand Bartimaeus the equivalent of a blue juice box and assume it met his need. He stopped, asked, and listened until Bartimaeus could name what he truly wanted: “Rabbi, I want to see.”\nZacchaeus Wanted to See Jesus. Jesus Saw Zacchaeus.\nZacchaeus was short, so when Jesus came through Jericho, he climbed a sycamore-fig tree to get above the crowd. He was a wealthy chief tax collector who wanted to see who Jesus was.\nThen something remarkable happened. Jesus reached the place. He looked up. The man trying to see Jesus discovered that Jesus saw him first.\nJesus called him down and announced that He would stay at Zacchaeus's house. The crowd grumbled because Jesus had chosen to associate with a man they regarded as a sinner. But the encounter changed Zacchaeus. He pledged generosity to the poor and restitution to people he had wronged.\nFor the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.\nLuke 19:10 (NIV)\nThe crowd saw a tax collector. Jesus saw Zacchaeus. The crowd saw his reputation. Jesus saw the man behind it.\nWe do something similar today. We call someone homeless, addict, prisoner, immigrant, alcoholic, poor, mentally ill, divorced, unemployed, difficult, or lost. Sometimes our labels describe something true about a person's circumstances. But no circumstance contains the entirety of a human being. Jesus sees beyond the label.\n“Daughter”\nA woman who had been subject to bleeding for twelve years had suffered greatly under many doctors and spent all she had, yet grew worse. She came behind Jesus in the crowd and touched his cloak, believing she would be healed. Immediately her bleeding stopped.\nJesus realized that power had gone out from him and asked, “Who touched my clothes?” His disciples pointed to the crowd pressing around him, but Jesus kept looking around to see who had done it.\nThe woman came, fell at his feet, and told him the whole truth. He said to her, “Daughter, your faith has healed you. Go in peace and be freed from your suffering.”\nMark 5:25–34 (NIV, condensed)\nJesus initially did not know who had touched Him. Yet He did not simply acknowledge that a healing had occurred and continue walking. He looked for her. He wanted the person.\nAnd when she finally stood before Him, Jesus did not call her “woman.” He called her Daughter. There is intimacy in that word. There is identity. There is belonging. There is dignity.\nShe came hoping to anonymously receive healing. Jesus gave her something more. He saw her.\nSeeing the Face of Jesus\nMy mother-in-law, Jerri Savuto, understood something about this kind of love. Jerri had wanted to be a missionary since she was a young girl growing up in New Mexico. She became a nurse, married Bill Savuto, and together they spent significant portions of their lives serving through missions in Native American communities in the southwestern United States, Belize, Nigeria, and eventually Kenya, where they served at Maua Methodist Hospital.\nDuring one of the times my wife, Corrie, and I visited them in Kenya, Jerri had become involved in helping local patients who could not afford their hospital bills. The hospital would treat emergency patients, but patients could face difficulty leaving until arrangements had been made for payment. Corrie and I contributed some of our tithe money to help Jerri assist people caught in that situation.\nAfter we returned home, a father brought his adult daughter to the hospital with an emergency. She received the treatment she needed, but the family had no money to pay the bill. Jerri learned what had happened and quietly used donated funds to pay the woman's bill. The father and daughter had never met Jerri and did not know she was doing anything for them.\nAfter taking care of the bill, Jerri walked toward her office in an area where patients and their families normally were not supposed to be. She turned a corner and saw a man walking toward her. It was the father. Somehow, he knew. He thanked her for what she had done.\nWhen Jerri later told the story, she described looking into his face and feeling that she had seen the face of Jesus. Perhaps that is exactly what Jesus meant.\nWhen Did We See You?\nAll of these stories lead toward one of Jesus' most challenging teachings.\nWhen the Son of Man comes in his glory, he will separate the people as a shepherd separates sheep from goats. The King will say to those on his right that they gave him food when he was hungry, drink when he was thirsty, welcome when he was a stranger, clothing when he needed it, care when he was sick, and companionship when he was in prison.\nThey will ask, “Lord, when did we see you?”\nThe King will reply, “Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.” He will also say that whatever was not done for one of the least of these was not done for him.\nMatthew 25:31–46 (NIV, condensed)\nThe question running through this passage is not merely, “What did we do?” It is also, “When did we see you?”","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:4","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"Jesus identifies Himself so closely with hungry people, strangers, those needing clothing, the sick, and prisoners that our response to them becomes a response to Him. Seeing people is not merely an act of kindness. It is an encounter with Christ.\nJerri looked into the face of a grateful father in Kenya and saw the face of Jesus. I sat beside John on a bench in Athens and discovered the sacred gift of seeing and being seen. Hagar learned that the God of the universe knew her misery and called her by name. And a blue juice box taught me one of the most basic truths about our shared humanity: every person has preferences, desires, dignity, and a story worthy of being seen.\nThe world may see a problem. Jesus sees a person. The world may see a category. Jesus sees a name. The world may see an interruption. Jesus sees someone worth stopping for.\nLearning to See\nTo truly see another person does not always require a dramatic act. Sometimes it begins with eye contact. A name. A question asked without rushing the answer. A willingness to sit on a bench. A recognition that the person receiving help still has preferences, dignity, agency, and a story.\nFood matters. Clothing matters. Housing, medical care, safety, and justice matter. Jesus met physical needs, and His followers are called to do the same. But people also hunger for dignity, relationship, and the assurance that they have not disappeared from the human family.\nWe cannot see every person with the perfect, limitless attention of God. We will miss people. We will become distracted. We will sometimes discover, as I did with the woman and the juice box, that our service has been mixed with pride or impatience. But grace allows us to recognize our blindness and learn a better way.\nThe question is not simply whether people pass through our field of vision. The priest and the Levite saw the wounded man. Thousands saw John on his bench. I saw the woman holding out her hand for a drink. The deeper question is whether we allow another person's humanity to reach us.\nWhen we truly see, compassion becomes possible. We draw near. We listen. We learn names. We surrender the comfort of our assumptions. We let another person's need interrupt our schedule, and we allow that person to give something to us in return.\nBecause true seeing is never entirely one-directional. John did not merely receive my attention; he offered me his trust. The woman with the juice box gave me a lesson I still carry. The people we think we are serving often become the people through whom God serves, teaches, and changes us.\nThe One Who Sees Us\nAt the foundation of all Christian compassion is this truth: we have already been seen. God saw Hagar in the wilderness. Jesus saw individuals within crowds. He saw Bartimaeus when others wanted him silent. He saw Zacchaeus behind a reputation. He looked for the woman who hoped to remain anonymous, and He called her Daughter.\nHe sees us too—not as categories, achievements, failures, or problems to be solved, but as people known and loved. We do not learn to see others in order to earn His attention. We learn to see because His attention has already found us.\nAnd when we see the hungry, the stranger, the lonely, the sick, the prisoner, the refugee, the neighbor, the family member, or the person everyone else has learned to overlook, Jesus tells us that we are also seeing Him.\nSo perhaps the question we should carry into our streets, churches, workplaces, and homes is not simply, “Who needs my help?” It is more personal and more demanding: Who is asking, whether aloud or silently, ‘Do you see me?’\nMay we become people who can answer with more than a glance. May we stop. May we listen. May we draw near. May we love with our heart, soul, strength, and mind.\nAnd may we discover, in the faces of those we finally learn to see, the face of the One who has always seen us.\nContinue the theme\nOne idea, more than one form.\nThis article is part of the Seeing People collection. Continue with the companion song by @PapaOdin and Brent D Kern, the official music video by Brent D Kern, or Brent D Kern’s sermon.\nListen to the song →Watch the video →Listen to the sermon →Explore all resources\nVerse 1\nDo you see me?\nDo you see me?\nNot the label on my back,\nNot the worst thing that I’ve done.\nDo you see me?\nYou pass me on the corner like I’m part of the street,\nEyes on the pavement so you don’t have to meet\nThe face of a stranger, the weight in his eyes.\nA thousand people walking, but nobody asked why.\nYou see the damage, you see the shame,\nBut there’s a story underneath my name.\nA heart that still remembers being loved,\nA soul that’s worth more than what I’ve become.\nChorus\nDo you see me?\nNot the label, not the need,\nNot another problem you don’t have time to read.\nDo you see me?\nI’m a soul beneath this skin.\nI’m somebody’s son, somebody’s friend.\nThe world sees a stranger,\nBut Jesus sees me.\nVerse 2\nShe held out her hand and I handed her blue.","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/index.html:5","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/","kind":"website","body":"She shook her head like, “That’s not what I want from you.”\nI thought, “After everything I’m giving away,\nWhy can’t you just take it and be grateful today?”\nThen somebody told me she wanted red,\nAnd suddenly I saw her—not a refugee, but a life instead.\nA favorite color, people she loved,\nA story and a home she was dreaming of.\nI thought I came to give her what she needed,\nBut God showed me how blind I’d been.\nI was looking straight at her face,\nBut I never really saw her.\nChorus\nDo you see me?\nNot the label, not the need,\nNot another problem you don’t have time to read.\nDo you see me?\nI’m a soul beneath this skin.\nI’m somebody’s son, somebody’s friend.\nThe world sees a stranger,\nBut Jesus sees me.\nBridge\nThe crowd saw a beggar; Jesus saw a man.\nThey said, “Be quiet.” Jesus said, “Come here.”\nShe reached for His clothes and tried to disappear,\nBut Jesus stopped walking; He turned and saw her there.\nOne word: “Daughter.”\nYou are not invisible.\nFinal Lift\nDo You see me?\nNow I know the answer is yes.\nBefore I ever called Your name,\nYou knew me at my worst.\nOh yeah, You see me:\nEvery scar and every sin,\nEvery place I’ve run away,\nEvery place I’ve ever been.\nAnd if You really see me,\nThen teach me how to see\nPast the labels, past the failure,\nPast what the world believes.\nWhen everybody sees a problem,\nLet me see a person.\nWhen everybody keeps on walking,\nLet me be the one who stops.\nWhen somebody whispers,\n“Do you see me?”\nLet my answer be,\n“Yeah, I see you,\nAnd you’re not alone.”\nOutro\nThe God who sees me\nIs teaching me to see.","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/do-you-see-me/"},{"id":"site:resources/index.html:0","title":"Resources for the Journey","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"A path through Athens beneath a warm sky\nPrepare. Serve. Reflect.\nResources for the Journey\nThoughtful resources for going with faith, serving with humility, and carrying hope home.\nMore than a reading list\nLet the journey begin before you leave.\nMission begins long before a plane takes off, and its lessons should continue long after we return home. This collection brings together articles, sermons, devotionals, songs, videos, and other trusted resources that can help us prepare our hearts, understand the people and places we encounter, serve without assuming, and reflect on what God may be teaching us.\nSome resources belong together. An article may grow into a sermon, a devotional may help you sit with the same idea more personally, and a song or book may offer another way into the conversation. Look for the Part of a collection label to follow those connections and explore a theme from more than one direction.\nExplore\nFind something for your next step.\nLoading resources…\nSearch resources\nFilter by type\nNo resources match that search and filter.","context":"Resources for the Journey /resources/"},{"id":"site:schedule/index.html:0","title":"Schedule a Conversation","url":"/schedule/","kind":"website","body":"Start with a conversation\nChoose a time that works for you.\nWhether you are considering a journey, exploring an internship, or imagining a ministry partnership, the next step can begin with a simple conversation.\nSchedule\nLet’s talk about what is stirring.\nSelect an available time below for a free conversation with Brent. There is no pressure and no obligation—just room to ask questions and consider a wise next step.\n30 minutes · Online\nHaving trouble with the scheduler? Open Calendly in a new tab.","context":"Schedule a Conversation /schedule/"},{"id":"site:stories/index.html:0","title":"A moment worth staying with","url":"/stories/","kind":"website","body":"The red juice box · 01 / 02Skip to the main site ↗\nFrom Brent’s story · Athens, Greece\nIt seemed like\nsuch a small thing.\nThe place where this story happened.\nOne of many places hope can lead.\nOne evening in Athens, I handed a woman a sandwich and a juice box. She accepted the food, but pushed the drink back.\nOur guide helped me understand: she wanted a red juice box. All I had left were blue ones.\nI found myself wondering why she couldn’t just be grateful.\nBack to the invitationStay with the story↗\nThe red juice box · 02 / 02Skip to the main site ↗\nFrom Brent’s story · Athens, Greece\nThen Amanda\nsaid my name.\nThe place where this story happened.\nOne of many places hope can lead.\nBack at the apartment, I told the team what had happened. Amanda listened.\n“Brent… she wanted a red juice box.”\nShe had to say it twice. This woman had preferences. Wants. A life of her own. I had offered her something to drink, but I hadn’t really seen her.\nPrevious moment↗Where could this lead?↗\nAn evening with John · 01 / 02Skip to the main site ↗\nFrom Brent’s story · Athens, Greece\nA little time\non a bench.\nThe place where this story happened.\nOne of many places hope can lead.\nI met John during an evening walk in Athens. While the group continued, I sat beside him and talked.\nThe next evening, I went back. This time, we stayed together longer. He told me about his life, his daughter, and the bench he called his office.\nBack to the invitationStay with the story↗\nAn evening with John · 02 / 02Skip to the main site ↗\nFrom Brent’s story · Athens, Greece\nAn honest\nanswer.\nThe place where this story happened.\nOne of many places hope can lead.\nAs I was leaving, I told John to take care of himself.\n“Brent, the problem isn’t taking care of myself. The problem is loneliness.”\nThousands of people could pass him every day. I was beginning to understand what it meant to stop.\nPrevious moment↗Where could this lead?↗\nAnother moment to sit withThe red juice boxAn evening with John","context":"A moment worth staying with /stories/"},{"id":"site:students/index.html:0","title":"College and university partnerships","url":"/students/","kind":"website","body":"← Keep exploring\nLearning with a human context\nHelp learning become\nlived experience.\nExplore how a ministry placement could connect your students’ disciplines with purposeful work.\nWe begin with your students, their learning goals, and the structure your institution needs. Together, we can consider where ministry needs and educational goals align.\nTalk about your program↗See the internship experience ↗\nA conversation to begin\nBuild the experience thoughtfully\nLearning outcomes and suitable work\nSupport, supervision, and communication\nTiming and institutional requirements\nStart with a question. We’ll work through the possibilities together.","context":"College and university partnerships /students/"},{"id":"site:trip/index.html:0","title":"Upcoming Trip","url":"/trip/","kind":"website","body":"Upcoming journey\nLoading trip details…\nHope Sojourns\nThis trip is not public yet.\nExplore the mission opportunities that are currently available.\nExplore trips\nUpcoming journey\nHope Sojourns trip\nDatesLocationStatusTeamI'm interested in this trip\nThe journey\nA specific place. A specific time. A thoughtful invitation.\nTake the next step\nStart with a conversation.\nThe first interest form is intentionally short. A Hope Sojourns leader will follow up before asking for full traveler details.\nShare my interest","context":"Upcoming Trip /trip/"},{"id":"site:brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf:page1:0","title":"Hope Sojourns Trifold Brochure Full Page — page 1","url":"/brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf#page=1","kind":"document","body":"A DIFFERENT WAY TO GO\nNot mission tourism.\nMeaningful partnership.\nThe best service begins with humility. Hope Sojourns brings\nwilling people alongside trusted local leaders and the good\nwork already underway.\nLISTEN FIRST\nLearn the people, place, strengths, and priorities before\ndeciding what help should look like.\nSERVE PRACTICALLY\nBring useful skills and willing hands to work identified by local\nministry partners.\nCARRY IT HOME\nReflect, tell stories with dignity, keep relationships, and let the\njourney reshape everyday service.\nCome as an individual, church, school, ministry team, or\norganization.\nHopeSojourns.com\nWAYS TO JOIN THE WORK\nA place for willing hands.\nNo single skill defines a sojourner. Opportunities are shaped with\ntrusted local partners and may include:\nHospitality, outreach, and encouragement.\nChildren, youth, and community activities.\nPractical projects, communications, and ministry support.\nIndividuals and teams are equally welcome.\nYou do not need every detail settled before beginning the conversation.\nYour presence should strengthen - never replace - local leadership.\nCOME READY TO LISTEN, LEARN + SERVE.\nBEGIN THE CONVERSATION\nWhere might hope lead\nyou?\nTell us what is stirring - joining a team, bringing a group,\nexploring an internship, or building a ministry partnership.\nExplore. Ask. Imagine.\nCurrent journeys, developing opportunities, giving information,\nand the easiest way to start a conversation are all online:\nHopeSojourns.com\nSCHEDULE A CONVERSATION\nHopeSojourns.com/schedule/\nHope Sojourns works with Christian Steps Ministries to create\npurposeful opportunities for faith, service, learning, and partnership.\nGO WITH HOPE. SERVE WITH FAITH.\nSUPPORT THE JOURNEY\nDonate to Hope Sojourns and help equip purposeful, partner-led\njourneys of faith, service, and lasting relationship.\nHopeSojourns.com/giving/\nAN OPEN INVITATION\nYou are invited\nto sojourn with us.\nTravel with purpose. Serve with humility. Return home\nseeing the world - and your place in it - differently.\nHopeSojourns.com\nMISSION JOURNEYS | INTERNSHIPS | GROUP TRIPS\nHALIFAX, ENGLAND","context":"Hope Sojourns Trifold Brochure Full Page — page 1 /brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf#page=1"},{"id":"site:brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf:page2:0","title":"Hope Sojourns Trifold Brochure Full Page — page 2","url":"/brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf#page=2","kind":"document","body":"OUR MISSION\nGo farther. Serve\ncloser.\nHope Sojourns brings willing people alongside\ntrusted local ministries - creating room to listen,\nlearn, encourage, and meet practical needs.\nWe create well-organized opportunities for people to serve\ncommunities and strengthen effective local ministries through\nshort-term journeys, extended placements, and group\npartnerships.\nPARTNER-LED\nLocal leaders identify real priorities and shape the work.\nPREPARED\nTeams receive thoughtful planning, spiritual preparation, and\npractical expectations.\nDIGNITY-CENTERED\nPeople are neighbors with names, agency, gifts, and stories -\nnever projects or photo opportunities.\nBUILT TO CONTINUE\nThe journey becomes a beginning for reflection, relationship,\nand service at home.\n\"The best service begins with humility.\"\nBEFORE, DURING + AFTER\nThoughtfully prepared.\nBEFORE: Clear roles, costs, expectations, and spiritual preparation.\nDURING: Serve under local direction with flexibility and cultural humility.\nAFTER: Reflect, share stories with dignity, and keep serving where you\nlive.\nFEATURED JOURNEYS\nAlready taking shape.\nJoin a planned journey - and check the website for new dates,\nopenings, and developing destinations.\nENGLAND\nOctober 10 - 17, 2026\nDISCOVERY TRIP\nMEXICO CITY, MEXICO*\nJanuary 9 - 14, 2027\nTEAM CONFIRMED\nMarch 13 - 18, 2027\nSpots available\nATHENS, GREECE\nJune 26-July 6, 2027\nSCHEDULED | Spots available | Pastoral planning\nATHENS, GREECE\nJuly 10-20, 2027\nTEAM CONFIRMED | Additional spots available\nAND THERE IS MORE\nShepherd of the Ozarks in November 2027, six developing Mexico\nCity journeys in 2027, Kenya, internships, and future partner-led\nopportunities.\n* Mexico City journeys leave from DFW.\nDates, availability, costs, roles, and partner plans are confirmed before enrollment or\npayment.\nSCHEDULE A CONVERSATION\nHopeSojourns.com/schedule/\nPLANNED + CUSTOM\nOne invitation. A world\nof possibilities.\n100+\ncountries available for additional trip planning\nChoose one of our preplanned journeys, or ask Hope Sojourns\nto shape an additional opportunity around your group, calling,\nskills, and a trusted local partner.\nInternational + domestic\nCustom journeys can be organized in more than 100 countries,\nalong with domestic service opportunities across the United\nStates.\nBRING YOUR GROUP\nChurches, schools, ministries, teams, and organizations can\nbegin with a conversation.\nBRING YOUR GIFTS\nWe help connect practical skills and willing service with needs\nidentified by local leaders.\nStart at HopeSojourns.com\nHopeSojourns.com/schedule/\nFAITHFUL STEWARDSHIP\nTransparent stewardship.\nHope Sojourns is committed to complete transparency about all funds\ncollected and spent.\nTrip payments, donations, partner support, travel costs, and\nadministrative expenses are recorded separately and explained clearly.\nQuestions are always welcome.","context":"Hope Sojourns Trifold Brochure Full Page — page 2 /brochure/Hope-Sojourns-Trifold-Brochure-Full-Page.pdf#page=2"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page1:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 1","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=1","kind":"document","body":"Brent D Kern\nPage 1\nSEEING PEOPLE\nDo You See Me?\nLearning to See People the Way Jesus Does\nArticle | Brent D Kern | August 9, 2026\nThere is a difference between looking at a person and truly seeing them.\nThere is a difference between looking at a person and truly seeing them.\nWe look at people every day. We pass them in grocery stores, sit beside them in waiting rooms, drive past\nthem at intersections, work alongside them, worship beside them, and scroll past their pictures on our\nphones. We see faces constantly.\nBut how often do we actually see the person?\nThat question has become increasingly important to me through mission work, particularly during two trips\nto Athens, Greece. In Athens I encountered refugees, people experiencing homelessness, immigrants, people\nstruggling with addiction, and people who simply seemed lost in a city where thousands of people walked past\nthem every day.\nI went there intending to serve. Instead, God began teaching me how to see. And one of the first lessons was\nuncomfortable.\nThe God Who Sees\nHagar may be one of Scripture's clearest examples. She was an Egyptian servant in Abraham and Sarah's\nhousehold. When Sarah could not have children, Hagar became caught in the consequences of decisions made\nby other people. She became pregnant with Abraham's child, and the relationship between Sarah and Hagar\ndeteriorated badly.\nEventually Hagar fled into the wilderness. She was a foreigner, a servant, a woman with little social power,\npregnant, alone, and running away. Yet in the wilderness, God found her.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 1 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=1"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page2:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 2","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=2","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 2\nThe angel of the Lord found Hagar near a spring in the desert; it was the spring that is beside the road to Shur.\nAnd he said, “Hagar, slave of Sarai, where have you come from, and where are you going?”\n“I'm running away from my mistress Sarai,” she answered.\nThen the angel of the Lord told her, “Go back to your mistress and submit to her.” The angel added, “I will\nincrease your descendants so much that they will be too numerous to count.”\nThe angel of the Lord also said to her: “You are now pregnant and you will give birth to a son. You shall name\nhim Ishmael, for the Lord has heard of your misery.”\nShe gave this name to the Lord who spoke to her: “You are the God who sees me,” for she said, “I have now seen\nthe One who sees me.”\nGenesis 16:7-13 (NIV)\nWhat an extraordinary name for God: the God who sees me. Hagar may have felt invisible to everyone around\nher. She was not invisible to God.\nGod did not merely see a pregnant servant beside a spring. He saw Hagar. He knew where she had been, what\nhad happened to her, where she was going, and her misery. He called her by name.\nThat truth should shape the way followers of Jesus see other people. If we worship the God who sees, should\nwe not become people who see?\nThree Men Saw Him. Only One Truly Saw Him.\nJesus develops this idea powerfully in the story we know as the Good Samaritan.\nAn expert in the law asked Jesus, “What must I do to inherit eternal life?” Jesus pointed him to the commands to\nlove God with all his heart, soul, strength, and mind, and to love his neighbor as himself. Wanting to justify\nhimself, the man asked, “And who is my neighbor?”\nJesus told of a man attacked by robbers and left half dead. A priest came down the road and, when he saw the\nman, passed by on the other side. A Levite also came, saw him, and passed by. But a Samaritan came where the\nman was and, when he saw him, took pity on him. He bandaged the man's wounds, brought him to an inn, cared\nfor him, and paid for his continued care.\nLuke 10:25-35 (NIV, condensed)\nThe language is striking. The priest saw him. The Levite saw him. The Samaritan saw him. So who actually\nsaw the man?\nAll three saw a body beside the road. Only one allowed what he saw to become compassion. Only one moved\ncloser, touched the wounded man, interrupted his journey, spent his resources, and treated the wounded\nstranger as though his life mattered.\nThat is the difference between looking and seeing. True seeing changes something in us.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 2 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=2"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page3:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 3","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=3","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 3\nPerhaps that is what it takes to truly see someone. We see with our heart. We see with our soul. We see with\nour strength. We see with our mind. We see because we love.\nShe Wanted a Red One\nIn July 2025, I traveled to Athens on what I consider my first official mission trip. I had participated in service\nprojects before and had certainly done work in the name of Jesus, but this was different. We had intentionally\ntraveled somewhere for the purpose of serving people and sharing the love of Christ.\nOne evening our team walked through Athens distributing food to refugees and people experiencing\nhomelessness. We carried sandwiches, chocolate, and small juice boxes. Some of the juice boxes were blue.\nOthers were red.\nI approached a refugee woman who did not speak English and offered her a sandwich and some chocolate.\nThen I handed her a blue juice box. She immediately shook her finger. “No, no, no!”\nOur guide explained that she wanted a red juice box. I looked through my bag. I did not have any red ones left.\nShe continued insisting that she wanted red.\nInternally, my attitude began to change. I remember thinking how ridiculous the situation seemed. We were\nwalking through the city giving away food and drinks. Everything was free. Why should the color of the juice\nbox matter? My attitude became something like: You get what you get. You should be grateful for what we are\ngiving you. Eventually she walked away without a drink because she would not take the blue one.\nLater that evening our team gathered to talk about the day. When it was my turn, I told everyone about the\nencounter. I told it almost as though the woman's behavior demonstrated how unreasonable people could be.\nOne of the women on our team listened and then said something simple. “Brent, you don't understand. She\nwanted a red one.” I responded, “I know. She wanted a red one.” Then she said it again: “Brent. She wanted a\nred one.”\nSomething changed in me. Suddenly I understood. The issue had never really been the juice box. The issue\nwas that I had stopped seeing the woman.\nSomewhere in my thinking, she had become a category instead of a person: refugee, hungry person, someone\nreceiving charity. I had unconsciously placed myself above her. I was the person with something to give, and\nshe was the person who ought to gratefully accept whatever I chose to hand her.\nBut she was not merely “a refugee.” She was a woman. She had preferences, memories, fears, hopes, things\nshe liked and things she disliked. She probably had family members she loved and people she missed. She had\nexperienced happiness and disappointment. She had a personality. She had a story. Just like me.\nAnd on that particular evening, she wanted a red juice box. It was such a small thing, yet it exposed something\nsignificant in me. I had looked directly at her without truly seeing her.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 3 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=3"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page4:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 4","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=4","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 4\nJesus Saw the Crowd - and the People Inside It\nJesus went through all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, proclaiming the good news of the\nkingdom and healing every disease and sickness. When he saw the crowds, he had compassion on them, because\nthey were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd. Then he said to his disciples, “The harvest is\nplentiful but the workers are few. Ask the Lord of the harvest, therefore, to send out workers into his harvest\nfield.”\nMatthew 9:35-38 (NIV)\nJesus saw crowds differently than we do. We tend to turn crowds into statistics: hundreds, thousands,\nmillions; refugees, homeless populations, prisoners, migrants, poor communities, the uninsured, the\naddicted, the elderly, the grieving.\nStatistics can help us understand the scale of suffering, but they can also unintentionally hide the people\ninside the numbers. Jesus could look at a crowd and somehow never lose the individual. Matthew does not\nmerely say Jesus observed a large number of people. He saw them. He understood that they were harassed\nand helpless. And when Jesus saw them, He had compassion.\nThat pattern appears repeatedly throughout His ministry: Jesus saw. Jesus understood. Jesus had\ncompassion. Jesus responded. The question for us is whether we are willing to do the same.\nThe Loneliness of Being Invisible\nI returned to Athens in June 2026. During another evening feeding walk, I met a man from England whom I\nwill call John. He had been living homeless in Greece for more than twenty years.\nAfter giving him some food and something to drink, I began talking with him. The rest of our team needed to\ncontinue the feeding walk, but they encouraged me to stay. They would come back for me later. So I sat and\ntalked with John.\nWhen the group returned, I asked whether I could pray for him. He allowed me to. Later our guide, Artur, told\nme that John normally did not invite people into his life that way.\nThe following evening we went out again. Before we started, I told Artur, “I want to find John.” We found him\nsitting on the park bench he jokingly called his “office.” This time I stayed for about an hour.\nWe talked - not ministry-talk, and not a carefully constructed evangelistic presentation. We simply talked like\ntwo human beings. John told me about his life. I learned how he survived, about his nightly routine of finding\nfood and hoping for enough money for a glass of wine, about his distrust of governments and authority, and\nabout his former wife, his daughter, and his grandchildren.\nFor an hour, the man people walked past every day was not merely a homeless man sitting on a bench. He was\nJohn.\nEventually I had to leave. As I stood to go, I told him, “John, I am going to try to come back and see you again\nthis week. But until then, please take care of yourself.” His response has stayed with me ever since. He told me\nthat taking care of himself was never really the problem. The real problem was the loneliness.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 4 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=4"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page5:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 5","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=5","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 5\nJohn sat beside a path traveled by thousands of people. People saw him every day. Some probably gave him\nmoney. Others gave him food. Some hurried around him. Others deliberately avoided eye contact. Thousands\nof eyes saw someone sitting on that bench. But how many people actually saw John?\nFor that hour, I had an opportunity to see him. But something else happened too. John allowed me to see him.\nHe became vulnerable. He shared pieces of himself that he could have kept hidden. And as we talked, he saw\nme too.\nThat is part of what makes genuine human connection so powerful. I saw him. He allowed himself to be seen.\nHe saw me. And I allowed myself to be seen. For a little while, neither of us was a category. We were simply\ntwo people sitting together on a bench.\nThe Bible is filled with people longing to see and to be seen. Moses longed to see God. Thomas wanted to see\nevidence before he could believe. People repeatedly asked Jesus for signs. But Scripture also reveals\nsomething even more profound: before we ever begin searching for God, God sees us.\nJesus Stopped\nAs Jesus and his disciples, together with a large crowd, were leaving Jericho, a blind man named Bartimaeus\nwas sitting by the roadside begging. Hearing that Jesus was passing, he shouted, “Jesus, Son of David, have\nmercy on me!” Many rebuked him and told him to be quiet, but he shouted all the more.\nJesus stopped and said, “Call him.” When Bartimaeus came, Jesus asked, “What do you want me to do for you?”\n“Rabbi, I want to see,” he said.\n“Go,” Jesus replied, “your faith has healed you.” Immediately he received his sight and followed Jesus along the\nroad.\nMark 10:46-52 (NIV, condensed)\nMark prepares us for this encounter with a striking contrast. Just before Bartimaeus appears, James and John\nask Jesus for positions of honor (Mark 10:35-45). Jesus asks them, “What do you want me to do for you?” A\nfew verses later, He asks Bartimaeus exactly the same question. James and John ask for status. Bartimaeus\nasks for sight. The disciples can see Jesus with their eyes, yet they still misunderstand the road of sacrifice and\nservice He is traveling. Bartimaeus, though physically blind, recognizes Jesus as the Son of David and follows\nHim along the road.\nEven his name adds meaning to the encounter. Bartimaeus means “son of Timaeus,” and the name Timaeus\ncarries the sense of being honored or valued. Yet this “son of the honored one” is sitting beside the road as a\ndishonored man - a blind beggar whom the crowd attempts to silence. The crowd treats him as an\ninterruption. Jesus stops, calls him forward, listens to him, and publicly recognizes his faith. Jesus sees the\nhonor and value in the man others have dismissed.\nTwo words in that story deserve our attention: Jesus stopped. There was a crowd. There was noise. Jesus was\ntraveling somewhere. The people around Bartimaeus considered him an interruption and told him to be quiet.\nBut Jesus stopped.\nThe crowd heard noise. Jesus heard a person. The crowd saw a beggar. Jesus saw Bartimaeus.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 5 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=5"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page6:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 6","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=6","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 6\nThe crowd’s response echoes the priest and the Levite in Jesus’ story. They encounter a suffering person, but\nthey do not allow his need to interrupt their journey. They create distance - this time by trying to silence him.\nJesus responds as the Samaritan did. He allows another person’s need to interrupt His journey. He stops, calls\nBartimaeus closer, listens to him, and responds with mercy. The crowd sees an interruption beside the road.\nJesus sees a neighbor.\nThen Jesus asked an interesting question: “What do you want me to do for you?” The need might have seemed\nobvious, but Jesus allowed Bartimaeus to speak. He gave him dignity. He let the man describe his own need.\nSometimes seeing people means resisting the temptation to decide who they are and what they need before we\nhave listened to them. Sometimes seeing means stopping. Sometimes it means asking. Sometimes it means\nlistening.\nJesus did not hand Bartimaeus the equivalent of a blue juice box and assume it met his need. He stopped,\nasked, and listened until Bartimaeus could name what he truly wanted: “Rabbi, I want to see.”\nZacchaeus Wanted to See Jesus. Jesus Saw Zacchaeus.\nZacchaeus was short, so when Jesus came through Jericho, he climbed a sycamore-fig tree to get above the\ncrowd. He was a wealthy chief tax collector who wanted to see who Jesus was.\nThen something remarkable happened. Jesus reached the place. He looked up. The man trying to see Jesus\ndiscovered that Jesus saw him first.\nJesus called him down and announced that He would stay at Zacchaeus's house. The crowd grumbled because\nJesus had chosen to associate with a man they regarded as a sinner. But the encounter changed Zacchaeus. He\npledged generosity to the poor and restitution to people he had wronged.\nFor the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.\nLuke 19:10 (NIV)\nThe crowd saw a tax collector. Jesus saw Zacchaeus. The crowd saw his reputation. Jesus saw the man behind\nit.\nWe do something similar today. We call someone homeless, addict, prisoner, immigrant, alcoholic, poor,\nmentally ill, divorced, unemployed, difficult, or lost. Sometimes our labels describe something true about a\nperson's circumstances. But no circumstance contains the entirety of a human being. Jesus sees beyond the\nlabel.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 6 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=6"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page7:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 7","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=7","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 7\n“Daughter”\nA woman who had been subject to bleeding for twelve years had suffered greatly under many doctors and spent\nall she had, yet grew worse. She came behind Jesus in the crowd and touched his cloak, believing she would be\nhealed. Immediately her bleeding stopped.\nJesus realized that power had gone out from him and asked, “Who touched my clothes?” His disciples pointed to\nthe crowd pressing around him, but Jesus kept looking around to see who had done it.\nThe woman came, fell at his feet, and told him the whole truth. He said to her, “Daughter, your faith has healed\nyou. Go in peace and be freed from your suffering.”\nMark 5:25-34 (NIV, condensed)\nJesus initially did not know who had touched Him. Yet He did not simply acknowledge that a healing had\noccurred and continue walking. He looked for her. He wanted the person.\nAnd when she finally stood before Him, Jesus did not call her “woman.” He called her Daughter. There is\nintimacy in that word. There is identity. There is belonging. There is dignity.\nShe came hoping to anonymously receive healing. Jesus gave her something more. He saw her.\nSeeing the Face of Jesus\nMy mother-in-law, Jerri Savuto, understood something about this kind of love. Jerri had wanted to be a\nmissionary since she was a young girl growing up in New Mexico. She became a nurse, married Bill Savuto,\nand together they spent significant portions of their lives serving through missions in Native American\ncommunities in the southwestern United States, Belize, Nigeria, and eventually Kenya, where they served at\nMaua Methodist Hospital.\nDuring one of the times my wife, Corrie, and I visited them in Kenya, Jerri had become involved in helping\nlocal patients who could not afford their hospital bills. The hospital would treat emergency patients, but\npatients could face difficulty leaving until arrangements had been made for payment. Corrie and I contributed\nsome of our tithe money to help Jerri assist people caught in that situation.\nAfter we returned home, a father brought his adult daughter to the hospital with an emergency. She received\nthe treatment she needed, but the family had no money to pay the bill. Jerri learned what had happened and\nquietly used donated funds to pay the woman's bill. The father and daughter had never met Jerri and did not\nknow she was doing anything for them.\nAfter taking care of the bill, Jerri walked toward her office in an area where patients and their families\nnormally were not supposed to be. She turned a corner and saw a man walking toward her. It was the father.\nSomehow, he knew. He thanked her for what she had done.\nWhen Jerri later told the story, she described looking into his face and feeling that she had seen the face of\nJesus. Perhaps that is exactly what Jesus meant.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 7 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=7"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page8:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 8","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=8","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 8\nWhen Did We See You?\nAll of these stories lead toward one of Jesus' most challenging teachings.\nWhen the Son of Man comes in his glory, he will separate the people as a shepherd separates sheep from goats.\nThe King will say to those on his right that they gave him food when he was hungry, drink when he was thirsty,\nwelcome when he was a stranger, clothing when he needed it, care when he was sick, and companionship when\nhe was in prison.\nThey will ask, “Lord, when did we see you?”\nThe King will reply, “Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine,\nyou did for me.” He will also say that whatever was not done for one of the least of these was not done for him.\nMatthew 25:31-46 (NIV, condensed)\nThe question running through this passage is not merely, “What did we do?” It is also, “When did we see you?”\nJesus identifies Himself so closely with hungry people, strangers, those needing clothing, the sick, and\nprisoners that our response to them becomes a response to Him. Seeing people is not merely an act of\nkindness. It is an encounter with Christ.\nJerri looked into the face of a grateful father in Kenya and saw the face of Jesus. I sat beside John on a bench\nin Athens and discovered the sacred gift of seeing and being seen. Hagar learned that the God of the universe\nknew her misery and called her by name. And a blue juice box taught me one of the most basic truths about\nour shared humanity: every person has preferences, desires, dignity, and a story worthy of being seen.\nThe world may see a problem. Jesus sees a person. The world may see a category. Jesus sees a name. The\nworld may see an interruption. Jesus sees someone worth stopping for.\nLearning to See\nTo truly see another person does not always require a dramatic act. Sometimes it begins with eye contact. A\nname. A question asked without rushing the answer. A willingness to sit on a bench. A recognition that the\nperson receiving help still has preferences, dignity, agency, and a story.\nFood matters. Clothing matters. Housing, medical care, safety, and justice matter. Jesus met physical needs,\nand His followers are called to do the same. But people also hunger for dignity, relationship, and the\nassurance that they have not disappeared from the human family.\nWe cannot see every person with the perfect, limitless attention of God. We will miss people. We will become\ndistracted. We will sometimes discover, as I did with the woman and the juice box, that our service has been\nmixed with pride or impatience. But grace allows us to recognize our blindness and learn a better way.\nThe question is not simply whether people pass through our field of vision. The priest and the Levite saw the\nwounded man. Thousands saw John on his bench. I saw the woman holding out her hand for a drink. The\ndeeper question is whether we allow another person's humanity to reach us.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 8 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=8"},{"id":"site:resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf:page9:0","title":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 9","url":"/resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=9","kind":"document","body":"DO YOU SEE ME?\nHOPE SOJOURNS\nBrent D Kern\nPage 9\nWhen we truly see, compassion becomes possible. We draw near. We listen. We learn names. We surrender\nthe comfort of our assumptions. We let another person's need interrupt our schedule, and we allow that\nperson to give something to us in return.\nBecause true seeing is never entirely one-directional. John did not merely receive my attention; he offered me\nhis trust. The woman with the juice box gave me a lesson I still carry. The people we think we are serving often\nbecome the people through whom God serves, teaches, and changes us.\nThe One Who Sees Us\nAt the foundation of all Christian compassion is this truth: we have already been seen. God saw Hagar in the\nwilderness. Jesus saw individuals within crowds. He saw Bartimaeus when others wanted him silent. He saw\nZacchaeus behind a reputation. He looked for the woman who hoped to remain anonymous, and He called her\nDaughter.\nHe sees us too - not as categories, achievements, failures, or problems to be solved, but as people known and\nloved. We do not learn to see others in order to earn His attention. We learn to see because His attention has\nalready found us.\nAnd when we see the hungry, the stranger, the lonely, the sick, the prisoner, the refugee, the neighbor, the\nfamily member, or the person everyone else has learned to overlook, Jesus tells us that we are also seeing\nHim.\nSo perhaps the question we should carry into our streets, churches, workplaces, and homes is not simply,\n“Who needs my help?” It is more personal and more demanding: Who is asking, whether aloud or silently, ‘Do\nyou see me?’\nMay we become people who can answer with more than a glance. May we stop. May we listen. May we draw\nnear. May we love with our heart, soul, strength, and mind.\nAnd may we discover, in the faces of those we finally learn to see, the face of the One who has always seen us.\nScripture passages are identified in the text. Condensed passages preserve the article's original wording and references.","context":"Do You See Me   Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does — page 9 /resources/do-you-see-me/Do You See Me - Learning to See People the Way Jesus Does.pdf#page=9"},{"id":"site:resource:hope-sojourns-brochure:0","title":"Hope Sojourns Brochure","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"Hope Sojourns Brochure\nA Printable Overview for Individuals and Groups\nShare the Hope Sojourns mission, approach, featured journeys, group opportunities, internships, and ways to begin a conversation.\nHope Sojourns","context":"Hope Sojourns Brochure /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:do-you-see-me:0","title":"Do You See Me?","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"Do You See Me?\nLearning to See People the Way Jesus Does\nExplore the article, reflective song written by @PapaOdin and Brent D Kern, official music video by Brent D Kern, and companion sermon together in one collection about seeing people with the compassion of Jesus.\nBrent D Kern and @PapaOdin","context":"Do You See Me? /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:have-you-ever-fed-jesus:0","title":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus?","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus?\nAn invitation to see Christ in the overlooked and hurting, keeping faces and dignity at the center of practical love.\nBrent D Kern","context":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus? /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:least-of-these-sermon:0","title":"The Least of These","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"The Least of These\nHave you ever fed Jesus? A message about recognizing Christ in people who are hungry, vulnerable, displaced, or overlooked.\nBrent D Kern","context":"The Least of These /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:have-you-ever-fed-jesus-devotional:0","title":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus? — Devotional","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus? — Devotional\nA brief devotional on seeing Jesus in the overlooked and letting compassion replace contempt.\nBrent D Kern","context":"Have You Ever Fed Jesus? — Devotional /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:leave-or-go:0","title":"Leave or Go","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"Leave or Go\nDoes God call us merely to leave a place, or does He call us to go toward another place and purpose? A helpful reflection for anyone discerning a journey of service.\nBrent D Kern","context":"Leave or Go /resources/"},{"id":"site:resource:i-walked-today-where-jesus-walked:0","title":"I Walked Today Where Jesus Walked","url":"/resources/","kind":"website","body":"I Walked Today Where Jesus Walked\nA reflective song about encountering the places and story of Jesus, inviting travelers to let sacred journeys deepen their faith.\nBrent D. Kern","context":"I Walked Today Where Jesus Walked /resources/"},{"id":"site:gallery:Athens 2025:0","title":"Athens 2025 photo captions","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"caption","body":"Athens 2025\nSofia and Nikos of Hellenic Ministries of Athens with members of our team\nMars Hill in Athens\nA gathering at the Albanian Church of Athens\nThe Parthenon on the Acropolis\nEntering the Acropolis through the Propylaea\nThe rocky shore on Aegina Island\nBrent and Corrie Kern, leaders with Christian Steps and Hope Sojourns, at the Acropolis\nHadrian’s Arch in Athens\nThe Corinth Canal\nThe original Olympic Stadium in Athens\nAncient Corinth and the Temple of Apollo\nThe mission team at the Parthenon","context":"Athens 2025 photo captions /past-trips/gallery/"},{"id":"site:gallery:Athens 2026:0","title":"Athens 2026 photo captions","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"caption","body":"Athens 2026\nFlying to Zurich\nStill 45 minutes from Athens and the changes are evident. We are heading to big city.\nThe team is completely together. We journeyed to Mars Hill and I got to teach tonight as well as lead the team in communion. What a blessing.\nThe view of the Acroplis from Mars Hill. This was most likely the view that Paul had during his sermon.\nOur team from Wisconsin, Colorado, and Texas. Back Row (left to right): Ellen, Brent, Brent, Josh, Hannah, Zoey, Matt and Bryan. Front Row: Michael, Amelia, and Emma\nView of Athens walking up to the Acropolis\nThe ancient Roman theater built in 161 AD named Odeon of Herodes Atticus. This theater is still used today. If you look close, you can see people working to set up a concernt for tonight.\nA view of the back side of the theater with the Parthenon looming over head.\nSome kind of fruit that was picked from a tree near where we played soccer today. One of the locals picked it and put it in his hat and handed it out to us. It was delicious. If you know what it was, please let me know.\nSome of the Sudanese Refugees listening to instructions on how we are going to play today, along with Josh and Matt from the team.\nMichael and Brent - Our blister boys. They got significant blisters from playing soccer in socks.\nOur team and some of the Albanian Church members.\nHomeless man from Afghanistan.\nThis man had just taken fentanyl and was completely passed out. We thought he might be dead. Our nurse said he had a breathing rate of 5 breaths per minute.\nThis man is recently on the streets.\nThis is an Iranian family of 10 living in this little corner.\nSomeone sleeps on this cardboard and there are 100s of needles in the grass from drug use.\nThe group I walked with on the first night with a beautiful sunset in Athens.\nAfter our walk, we had dinner in the beautiful restaurant. Everyone of us felt very odd to be eating like this when we were just on the streets giving out sandwiches to people and we are not in this space.\nThis is an image of a man (father) with his 4 year old and 6 year old children. We all wept at the sight, but I was able to find the beauty in a father protecting his children.\nOur team with the Albanian kids.\nMichael Hansen with Abanian interpreter during his sermon at the Albanian Church\nMembers of the Albanian Church in Sanctuary\nDeaconess of the Albanian Church\nGirls from our team with Albanians Teenage girls and lunch\nHomeless woman with her beautiful baby\nHomeless man that Arturo feeds every chance he can get.\nAthens mission day — June 7, 2026\nOur tour guide in Corinth - James. He is an amazing Pauline Scholar. What a joy to see Corinth with him again this year.\nName plate with Erastus mentioned in the June 8th update. This shows and proves that the Christian religion is and was not for the poor alone, it is for us all.\nTemple of Apollo\nThe Bema seat where Gaius (governor of Corinth) over saw Paul's trial. There is so much proof of this event happening. Paul stood right here, and would have been tied to this beating post if he had been guilty. Paul was beatened several times through his life. Every time he was not guilty but he rarely used his Roman citizenship to avoid a beating. Some believe he didn't use his Roman citizenship beacause of the guilt he felt for executing so many Christians.\nJosh was asking if he got the sunscreen rubbed in completely - He hadn't.\nView of the Gulf of Corinth from where we ate lunch today.\nBack in Athens, this is the actual basketball court where Giannis Antetokounmpo grew up and learned to play basketball. He is now an NBA champion and multi-time MVP.\nInside the New Start office with Dimitrius preparing to go on our first walk.\nAlmost everywhere you look in Athens, you see graffiti. Right on the edge of one of the most densely populated brothel district in all of Europe is this beautiful mural on the side of the building. You can see from the balconies that this is at least 60 feet high\nThe roof top areas of the Glocal Cafe for Refugees in Athens.\nOne of the very rare nice statements of graffiti in the city.\nRight in the middle of a very exclusive and expensive shopping district, there is this ancient tiny church.\nIt is near closing time and the busiest shopping street in Athens is still packed.\nWe had a ping pong tournament today on the Glocal Cafe rooftop. These are the first, second, and third place winners.\nA little fun to end this deep day. We found a statue in Corinth of our fearless leader, Bryan Doyle.\nThe team during our morning devotional.\nBrian, Michael, and Brent (Kern) talking with a refugee from Sudan. He was getting close to getting his EU passport and one night while sleeping on the streets, all of his papers were stolen and he is basically having to start over the process.\nHanding out new(ish) clothes to the refugees on the rooftop of the Glocal Cafe for Refugees.","context":"Athens 2026 photo captions /past-trips/gallery/"},{"id":"site:gallery:Athens 2026:1","title":"Athens 2026 photo captions","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"caption","body":"Refugees from at least 43 different countries sit around Natalie during some time at the park. They all really loved to draw and color.\nA picture of most of the group that went to the park.\nA street near Omonia (a section of Athens). There are about 50 brothels within 4 blocks of this picture.\nEvery red circle represents a location where there is at least 1 brothel and sometimes 4 or 5 in the same multi-story building. The blue #1 is near where our AirBNB apartment is. The blue #2 is near where Glocal Cafe is. There are at least 400 brothels within 4 miles of this location. This is the Victory Square district and it is one of the most populated brothel districts in Europe.\nThis is another distric near Omonia where there is a large concentration of brothels.\nAn abstract mural in the Onomia district.\nThis mural was painted in 2006 when Greece was going through a very difficult time which include national bankruptcy. The story behind the mural is, when people pray we put our hands toether and lift them to God. These are God's hands pleading for the people of Greece to repent and change their ways. This mural is right on the border of one of the largest brothel districts in Europe.\nThis is the view from Monistiraki Square. You can see the same chapel on the square, Mars Hills up and to the right, and the Acropolis up and to the left.\nAn active Archeological site in Athens near Montistiraki Square.\nBrent K and Ellen after our journey to buy a new Patio Umbrella for the Glocal Refugee Center.\nAthens Metropolitan Cathedral - This Church is so pretty and the square is amazing.\nInside the Athens Metropolitan Cathedral.\nInside the Athens Metropolitan Cathedral.\nInside the Athens Metropolitan Cathedral.\nOnboard the Ferry to Aegina Island\nMe and my Scooter\nBeautiful storm building over the Gulf of Aegina.\nBlack and White photo of Josh and Hannah where he proposed and she said, YES\nOur final lunch/dinner. We are toasting with Pineapple Fizz, a concoction I invented. They did not have any non-alcoholic sparkling wine for the engagement.\nTy and Brent on their HOGS on Aegina Island\nBrent K, Zoie, and Ty in a tiny car on the way to Athens airport.\nMy hotel came with a pool. Or maybe it is just a big bathtub. The jury is still out.\nI was able to set my AC to 18 degrees celsius. Ty and I had our room in Victoria Square at 17. It is so hot in here.\nMy last dinner in Greece. Chicken Souvlaki with potatoes, pita, Red Wine (they said it was red, it looks more like rose), bread with Olive Oil and Balsamic.\nBledi and I from the Albanian church. His wife Blerina Mile sent this to me today and I had to share it.\nIn Greece, you cannot check into a flight until they say you can check into a flight. We were put into several lines. Not sure the woman in charge knew what she was doing. She kept moving people around. It was quite funny.\nAnother image of check-in. The people working behind the counter sat down and stared at us for about 15 minutes before they let anyone check in. Maybe there are unions in Greece too?\nView from my apartment this morning. The bed was sooooo comfortable. I got a great night's sleep.\nI stayed in Spata Greece last night. I was NOT in SpaRta, it was at least close, so Bryan made this lovely image of me. I think that glasses really round out the image.","context":"Athens 2026 photo captions /past-trips/gallery/"},{"id":"site:gallery:SOTO Arkansas 2026:0","title":"SOTO Arkansas 2026 photo captions","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"caption","body":"SOTO Arkansas 2026\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 1\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 2\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 4\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 5\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 6\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 7\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 8\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 9\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 10\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 11\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 12\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 13\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 14\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 15\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 16\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 17\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 28, photo 18\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 29\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 29, photo 3\nShepherd of the Ozarks visit, July 30, photo 1\nThe Buffalo Conference Center and auditorium at Shepherd of the Ozarks\nThe original July 30 view from Shepherd of the Ozarks\nSunset over the road on the journey home\nRing-tailed lemurs at the animal exhibit\nAnimals gathered in the outdoor habitat at SOTO\nA peacock at the SOTO animal exhibit\nFeeding ring-tailed lemurs at SOTO\nTwo ring-tailed lemurs visiting up close\nRing-tailed lemurs enjoying a snack\nA visitor seated with several ring-tailed lemurs\nA visitor feeding a ring-tailed lemur","context":"SOTO Arkansas 2026 photo captions /past-trips/gallery/"},{"id":"site:gallery:Kenya 2018:0","title":"Kenya 2018 photo captions","url":"/past-trips/gallery/","kind":"caption","body":"Kenya 2018\nChristian Health Association of Kenya emblem\nWalking the hospital compound in Maua\nA chapel on the hospital grounds\nA tree-lined path through the hospital compound\nPreparing the ground for the new hospital wall\nWorking alongside local partners in Maua\nSharing a joyful moment with a local child\nSpending time and playing with children\nThe hospital wall project taking shape\nBuilding a safer boundary for the hospital compound\nStone and construction work at the hospital\nMaterials prepared for the new wall\nMission team members with a child in Kenya\nA gerenuk standing upright to browse leaves during safari\nAntelope on the Kenyan landscape\nGiraffes crossing the open country\nAn ostrich on safari\nA team photograph at Samburu Game Lodge\nWildlife encountered during safari\nLions beside the safari road\nTopi grazing on the plain\nElephants crossing the grassland\nA spotted hyena in the tall grass\nA wide view of migrating wildlife\nA quiet view from the safari lodge\nColorful textiles at a Kenyan market\nA warm welcome at the local market\nKenya Meteorological Services headquarters\nTeaching a devotional in Kenya","context":"Kenya 2018 photo captions /past-trips/gallery/"}];
